# Go files check out with LF on every platform.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is the Windows default, and it makes `gofmt -l` report
# every checked-out .go file as unformatted — so the owned format gate is red
# before anyone has edited anything. A gate that is always red is a gate
# nobody reads: that is how an unformatted test table survived eight pushes
# to main before CI was believed.
#
# The blobs in git are already LF. This only fixes what lands in the working
# copy, and gofmt itself only ever writes LF.
*.go text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF to run at all, on the box and in CI.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Data files check out with LF too, for the same reason and the same cost.
#
# The rule above pinned the source and left the data the source is generated
# from and compared against unpinned, so a fresh Windows clone still failed
# three packages before anyone had edited anything:
#
#   internal/names          names/names.json is the committed golden, compared
#                           byte for byte against what the emitter produces,
#                           and the emitter only ever writes LF
#   internal/registry/config catalog.yaml is embedded and matched on the
#                           literal "\n    free:\n"
#   internal/orgplan        loads that same embedded catalogue and patches it
#                           with byte substitutions to build its failure cases
#
# YAML parsing survives CRLF, so names/names.yaml is pinned for the artifacts
# it generates rather than for its own sake. The blobs in git are already LF:
# this changes no blob, only what lands in a working copy.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
